Tilted = tilt difference > 0.11 deg mod 90 (SLSQP leaves lattice pairs up to 0.08 deg apart; those are PAR corner points). "Loadable": max of lam_A + lam_B over the lam0 = 1 polytope > 1e-6; "necessarily loaded": min > 1e-6; "VV-free stress": a multiplier with every tilted VV row of the witness at zero.  EXCL-1: some loadable tilted VV pair fails the two-selection test (one of NLP(S_A), NLP(S_B) has no Fritz-John multiplier, all other rows kept).

**Genuine free FJ points (SETTLED / K0FREE / BOXK0, 1,000 witnesses incl. duplicates across the two lists) and the 24 optima (Trump, S02, S07, 21 records): every tilted VV contact is unloadable and a VV-free stress exists.**  The records square-38 and square-40 carry 45-deg diamonds whose tips sit at lattice notches of flat squares (loadable up to 0.71 / 1.00, both selections KKT) -- a VV contact geometrically, a vertex-on-a-composite-edge physically; their VV rows are not necessary.

## 2. NO-VV-IND check (the theorem's hypothesis on the data)

Tilted VV rows whose gradient is OUTSIDE the span of the other active rows (partner row included): 0, of which loadable: 0 (the theorem says 0).  Inside the span (dependent, over-braced): 82 -- every loadable tilted VV contact is of this kind.  The full active row set (A217: both axes of every tied pair, line rows) has rank 3n + 1 at 61 of 1393 witnesses.  Per consistent selection (select235, 31 witnesses): rows per selection [26, 45] against 34 coordinates, redundancy rows - rank [0, 12]; the VV row is independent of the other rows of the selection and of its partner in 2272 selection-instances, all at w125_crowd, w134_crowd, w135_crowd, w142_crowd -- exactly the witnesses the two-selection test excludes (NO-VV-IND applies there and forces the contact unloaded in those selections, which is what fails).

